## Bash Execution Rules

Always run each command as a separate Bash call.

Never chain commands using `&&`, `||`, `;`, or `|` in a single Bash call.

Shell operators break the tool's permission matching entirely — compound commands are unmatchable by design and always trigger an unavoidable approval prompt regardless of permission settings.

Bad:

```
cd "/Users/mtm/Documents/Obsidian Vault" && git add "file.md" && git commit -m "message"
```

Good:

```
cd "/Users/mtm/Documents/Obsidian Vault"
```

```
git add "file.md"
```

```
git commit -m "message"
```

Or, if the working directory is already correct, skip the `cd` entirely and issue each remaining command in its own call.

This applies to all commands, not just git.

When searching many directories, never loop in Bash invoking the tool once per directory.

Pass every path to a single `rg` invocation, which accepts multiple path arguments and searches them in parallel.

Keep exploratory commands bounded to roughly 20 seconds, and validate on a small subset before sweeping a large tree.

Never use `rm -rf <dir>` to clear a directory before a re-run, because that destroys the directory itself along with any Finder metadata, extended attributes, or folder-level settings attached to it.

Use `find <dir> -mindepth 1 -delete` instead, or `find <dir> -type f -delete` to leave subdirectory entries intact.
